<pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I5de997a4dee5449d578972e2f929c6e08c5dff67" commit="59ab1441ccbc9e0f15b4c9233587a0b69f969117" commit_message="npcx: modify the npcxspiflash little firmware to support UUT  The original ec_npcxspiflash lfw is used by the openocd to program SPI flash via Servo JTAG. In order to support UUT mode to program SPI flash, this CL modified the lfw with the following changes: 1. Rename the lfw ec_npcxflash to npcx_monitor to unify the naming. 2. The npcx_monitor will read the first 4 bytes from the area of monitor header. If the monitor identifies the first 4 bytes is a UUT tag, it will read parameters(SPI_OFFSET/IMAGE_SIZE) from the relative offset of monitor header. Otherwise, it will read parameter from the general register r0/r1 which will be restored by openocd script in advance. 3. Add monitor_hdr.c to generate the monitor header binary files (monitor_hdr_ro.bin/monitor_hdr_rw.bin)) automatically after compiled.  The memory layout to restore the reuqired binaries are listed below: ec firmware(RO/RW) - the start address of Code RAM area. monitor header - 0x200C3000 npcx_monitor - 0x200C3020  BRANCH=none BUG=none TEST=No build errors for &quot;make buildall&quot;. TEST=Follow instructions in CL:826763; make sure the ec firmware is updated and ec can boot up. CQ-DEPEND=CL:828341  Change-Id: I5de997a4dee5449d578972e2f929c6e08c5dff67 Signed-off-by: CHLin &lt;CHLIN56@nuvoton.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/826909 Commit-Ready: CH Lin &lt;chlin56@nuvoton.com&gt; Tested-by: CH Lin &lt;chlin56@nuvoton.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Randall Spangler &lt;rspangler@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="826909" owner_email="chlin56@nuvoton.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/09/826909/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="If622f83e2d2132d66b390bcee30c77e7a9d5f7bd" commit="6a8c513f935bf04bc9f48631084c24f59ad629c9" commit_message="util/openocd: modify the openocd srcipts for npcx UUT  In the CL:826909, the name and the restore address of ec_spiflash lfw are modified becaue of introducing the UUT. This CL modified the openocd scripts to follow the changes of the lfw.  BRANCH=none BUG=none TEST=Apply CL:826906 and &quot;BOARD=npcx7_evb make&quot;; Run cmd &quot;./util/flash_ec --board=npcx7_evb&quot; to flash ec image; Make sure the ec firmware can be updated with the new npcx_minotor lfw + this CL via JTAG on Servo v2.  Change-Id: If622f83e2d2132d66b390bcee30c77e7a9d5f7bd Signed-off-by: CHLin &lt;CHLIN56@nuvoton.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/828341 Commit-Ready: CH Lin &lt;chlin56@nuvoton.com&gt; Tested-by: CH Lin &lt;chlin56@nuvoton.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="828341" owner_email="chlin56@nuvoton.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="2" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/41/828341/2" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I810b2a68ab8d35e9cea6fa18f1eeafa10e7039ea" commit="52fa08d3bd97df3290c46f067f04cf18721c12ff" commit_message="poppy: Disable PULL_UP on BOARD_VERSION4  All shipping products will have board ID &gt;= 8, so the board ID strap 3 will never be left floating. In particular, board ID 8-15 will have an external pull-down on that pin, so leaving the internal pull-up enabled is detrimental to power.  BUG=b:64503543 BRANCH=none TEST=Boot poppy rev6.  Change-Id: I810b2a68ab8d35e9cea6fa18f1eeafa10e7039ea Signed-off-by: Nicolas Boichat &lt;drinkcat@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/845240 Tested-by: Ruben Rodriguez Buchillon &lt;coconutruben@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Ruben Rodriguez Buchillon &lt;coconutruben@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="845240" owner_email="drinkcat@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/40/845240/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I8f2503ce324e34b87b3bbfa3c509079357880c9e" commit="2655ce62d7983442cc014b69fa55f4acc54457df" commit_message="driver/bc12/pi3usb9281: Make a few functions static  Old oak boards that still use these functions have long been deprecated, let's make the unneeded functions static.  BRANCH=none BUG=b:35573263 TEST=make buildall -j =&gt; makes newsizes: up to 64 bytes saved on      a few boards.  Change-Id: I8f2503ce324e34b87b3bbfa3c509079357880c9e Signed-off-by: Nicolas Boichat &lt;drinkcat@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/848574 Reviewed-by: Shawn N &lt;shawnn@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="848574" owner_email="drinkcat@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/74/848574/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I27e6777fcec54b71887e268f28bd38845b70f202" commit="7ccda9bc78e39d04ab1972435ab24f8915ae4f1d" commit_message="chromeos-config: Adjust identity code to be more C++-ish  The current code accesses the string table directly to pull out the two strings that it needs. The table is typically fairly small (10 entries) so the cost of extracting it into a C++ vector is also fairly small:  - we now allocate all the strings, whereas previously we just did one big     alloc - we scan the table twice, calling strlen() on each string, as before - now when accessing a string, we don't need to scan the table each time  Overall it suggests that this CL will not slow things down appreciably. Empirical tests (with 100k runs) on coral show a time of 4840ms for the new code and 5010ms for the old code.  Adjust the code to use a vector, for better readability. Rename GetString() at the same time, for clarity.  BUG=chromium:796267, chromium:798771 TEST=FEATURES=test sudo -E emerge --nodeps chromeos-config-tools  Change-Id: I27e6777fcec54b71887e268f28bd38845b70f202 Signed-off-by: Simon Glass &lt;sjg@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/848642 Reviewed-by: Ben Chan &lt;benchan@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="848642" owner_email="sjg@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/42/848642/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-4.14" change_id="Ibf1dbfd30959d6ffe51d8b95e82185e57c75b1ce" commit="ddd5371ac9bfb05148cc1e198295b6eaf4b0889f" commit_message="FROMLIST: Bluetooth: Prevent stack info leak from the EFS element.  In the function l2cap_parse_conf_rsp and in the function l2cap_parse_conf_req the following variable is declared without initialization:  struct l2cap_conf_efs efs;  In addition, when parsing input configuration parameters in both of these functions, the switch case for handling EFS elements may skip the memcpy call that will write to the efs variable:  ... case L2CAP_CONF_EFS: if (olen == sizeof(efs)) memcpy(&amp;efs, (void *)val, olen); ...  The olen in the above if is attacker controlled, and regardless of that if, in both of these functions the efs variable would eventually be added to the outgoing configuration request that is being built:  l2cap_add_conf_opt(&amp;ptr, L2CAP_CONF_EFS, sizeof(efs), (unsigned long) &amp;efs);  So by sending a configuration request, or response, that contains an L2CAP_CONF_EFS element, but with an element length that is not sizeof(efs) - the memcpy to the uninitialized efs variable can be avoided, and the uninitialized variable would be returned to the attacker (16 bytes).  This issue has been assigned CVE-2017-1000410  BUG=chromium:797254 TEST=Build and run  Change-Id: Ibf1dbfd30959d6ffe51d8b95e82185e57c75b1ce Cc: Marcel Holtmann &lt;marcel@holtmann.org&gt; Cc: Gustavo Padovan &lt;gustavo@padovan.org&gt; Cc: Johan Hedberg &lt;johan.hedberg@gmail.com&gt; Cc: stable &lt;stable@vger.kernel.org&gt; Signed-off-by: Ben Seri &lt;ben@armis.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Greg Kroah-Hartman &lt;gregkh@linuxfoundation.org&gt; Signed-off-by: Guenter Roeck &lt;groeck@chromium.org&gt; (am from https://patchwork.kernel.org/patch/10102605/) Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/847703 Reviewed-by: Ben Chan &lt;benchan@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Miao-chen Chou &lt;mcchou@chromium.org&gt; (cherry picked from commit 0e09b163c371dafcedbdad49dde6963b746e5fed) Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/849435 " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="849435" owner_email="groeck@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/35/849435/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.18" change_id="Ibf1dbfd30959d6ffe51d8b95e82185e57c75b1ce" commit="0854af5a60f417ce09ba712089e54b8491aa6c47" commit_message="FROMLIST: Bluetooth: Prevent stack info leak from the EFS element.  In the function l2cap_parse_conf_rsp and in the function l2cap_parse_conf_req the following variable is declared without initialization:  struct l2cap_conf_efs efs;  In addition, when parsing input configuration parameters in both of these functions, the switch case for handling EFS elements may skip the memcpy call that will write to the efs variable:  ... case L2CAP_CONF_EFS: if (olen == sizeof(efs)) memcpy(&amp;efs, (void *)val, olen); ...  The olen in the above if is attacker controlled, and regardless of that if, in both of these functions the efs variable would eventually be added to the outgoing configuration request that is being built:  l2cap_add_conf_opt(&amp;ptr, L2CAP_CONF_EFS, sizeof(efs), (unsigned long) &amp;efs);  So by sending a configuration request, or response, that contains an L2CAP_CONF_EFS element, but with an element length that is not sizeof(efs) - the memcpy to the uninitialized efs variable can be avoided, and the uninitialized variable would be returned to the attacker (16 bytes).  This issue has been assigned CVE-2017-1000410  BUG=chromium:797254 TEST=Build and run  Change-Id: Ibf1dbfd30959d6ffe51d8b95e82185e57c75b1ce Cc: Marcel Holtmann &lt;marcel@holtmann.org&gt; Cc: Gustavo Padovan &lt;gustavo@padovan.org&gt; Cc: Johan Hedberg &lt;johan.hedberg@gmail.com&gt; Cc: stable &lt;stable@vger.kernel.org&gt; Signed-off-by: Ben Seri &lt;ben@armis.com&gt; Signed-off-by: Greg Kroah-Hartman &lt;gregkh@linuxfoundation.org&gt; Signed-off-by: Guenter Roeck &lt;groeck@chromium.org&gt; (am from https://patchwork.kernel.org/patch/10102605/) Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/847703 Reviewed-by: Ben Chan &lt;benchan@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Miao-chen Chou &lt;mcchou@chromium.org&gt; (cherry picked from commit 0e09b163c371dafcedbdad49dde6963b746e5fed) Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/849436 " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="849436" owner_email="groeck@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/36/849436/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><lkgm version="10276.0.0-rc3"/></manifest>